    "q-148351": {
      "dom_id": "step-q148351",
      "name": "Question 13",
      "problem": "The joint probability mass function of the random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.3$ | $0.15$ |\n| $X=4$ | $0.2$ | $0.35$ |\n\nFind $\\text{Var}[3X+4Y].$",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that if $X$ and $Y$ are random variables and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[aX+bY]={a}^{2}\\text{Var}[X]+{b}^{2}\\text{Var}[Y]+2ab\\,\\text{Cov}[X,Y].\n$$\n\nAlso, the variance of a random variable $X$ is determined by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2},\n$$\n\nand the covariance of two random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y.$ These are given by the row and column totals, respectively.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.3$ | $0.15$ | $0.45$ |\n| $X=4$ | $0.2$ | $0.35$ | $0.55$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.5$ | $0.5$ | $1$ |\n\nNow, we can find $\\text{Var}[X].$ If $X$ is defined on a sample space ${S}_{X},$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}x\\cdot {f}_{X}(x) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0.45+4\\cdot 0.55 \\\\  & =2.2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}{x}^{2}\\cdot {f}_{X}(x) \\\\  & ={0}^{2}\\cdot 0.45+{4}^{2}\\cdot 0.55 \\\\  & =8.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =8.8-{2.2}^{2} \\\\  & =3.96.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, if $Y$ is defined on a sample space ${S}_{Y},$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{y}}y\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0.5+2\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =1.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}] & ={\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{y}}{y}^{2}\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & ={1}^{2}\\cdot 0.5+{2}^{2}\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =2.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]-\\text{E}[Y]^{2} \\\\  & =2.5-{1.5}^{2} \\\\  & =0.25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find $\\text{Cov}[X,Y].$ We find $\\text{E}[XY]$ using the rule of the lazy statistician:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}xy\\cdot f(x,y) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 1\\cdot f(0,1)+0\\cdot 2\\cdot f(0,2)+4\\cdot 1\\cdot f(4,1)+4\\cdot 2\\cdot f(4,2) \\\\  & =0+0+4\\cdot 0.2+8\\cdot 0.35 \\\\  & =3.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the covariance formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[XY] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =3.6-2.2\\cdot 1.5 \\\\  & =0.3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[3X+4Y] & ={3}^{2}\\cdot 3.96+{4}^{2}\\cdot 0.25+2\\cdot 3\\cdot 4\\cdot 0.3 \\\\  & =35.64+4+7.2 \\\\  & =46.84.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
